/-!
# Prime divisors on locally factorial schemes are effective Cartier

Let `X` be a locally Noetherian scheme whose local rings are unique factorization domains, and let
`η` be a point of codimension one, the generic point of an integral closed subscheme
`Z = closure {η}` of codimension one. Then the vanishing ideal sheaf of `Z`, the ideal sheaf of its
reduced induced subscheme structure, is an effective Cartier divisor.

At a point `x` of `Z`, on an affine open `U = Spec A` containing `x`, the ideal of `Z` is the prime
`𝔮` of `A` corresponding to `η` (`vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_ideal`). In the local ring
`𝒪_{X,x} = A_𝔭` it generates a prime of height one, which is principal because `𝒪_{X,x}` is
factorial, generated by the image of some `a ∈ 𝔮`.
This local equation spreads out to a basic open neighbourhood `D(g)` of `x`, by
`Ideal.exists_notMem_smul_le_span_singleton_of_map_eq`, on which `𝔮` is generated by the
nonzerodivisor `a`. Away from `Z` the ideal is the unit ideal.

A regular local ring of dimension at most two is factorial
(`TauCeti.IsRegularLocalRing.uniqueFactorizationMonoid`). So on a regular scheme of dimension at
most two, such as a regular model of a curve over a discrete valuation ring, every integral
closed subscheme of codimension one is an effective Cartier divisor. In particular the
irreducible components of the special fibre of such a model, with their reduced structure, are
effective Cartier divisors, the input of the intersection numbers `(Cᵢ · Cⱼ)` of components.

Two distinct prime divisors meet properly: if `η ≠ η'` are codimension-one points and the closure
`Z'` of `η'` is an effective Cartier divisor, then `η ∉ Z'`, since a codimension-one point has no
proper codimension-one generization, so the inverse image of `Z'` on `Z` is an effective Cartier
divisor of the integral scheme `Z`. On a regular scheme of dimension at most two this holds for
every pair of distinct prime divisors, in both directions, which is the hypothesis under which
the intersection numbers `(Cᵢ · Cⱼ)` of distinct components are symmetric and computed by
`dim Γ(Cᵢ ∩ Cⱼ, 𝒪)`.

More generally, effective Cartier divisors cut out by a single function are sums of prime divisors.
For a nonzero global function `f` on a locally Noetherian integral scheme with factorial local
rings, the ideal sheaf generated by `f` is the product `∏ 𝒪_X(-closure {η})^{ord_η(f)}` over the
codimension-one points `η` at which `f` vanishes. For a regular model this is the decomposition
`𝒪_X(-X_s) = ∏ᵢ 𝒪_X(-Cᵢ)^{mᵢ}` of its special fibre.

## Main results

In the namespace `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData`:

* `isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton`: on a locally Noetherian scheme with
  factorial local rings, the closure of a codimension-one point is an effective Cartier divisor;
* `isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_isRegular`: the same on a regular scheme
  all of whose points have codimension at most two;
* `IsEffectiveCartier.comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_ne`: a prime divisor
  which is an effective Cartier divisor meets every other prime divisor properly;
* `isEffectiveCartier_comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_isRegular`: distinct
  prime divisors on a regular scheme of dimension at most two meet properly;
* `ofIdealTop_span_singleton_eq_prod_vanishingIdeal`: on a locally Noetherian integral scheme
  with factorial local rings, the ideal sheaf of a nonzero global function `f` is the product of
  the ideal sheaves of the prime divisors, raised to the orders of vanishing of `f`.

## References

* R. Hartshorne, *Algebraic Geometry*, Chapter II, Proposition 6.11: on a Noetherian integral
  separated scheme whose local rings are unique factorization domains, Weil divisors and Cartier
  divisors agree.
* The Stacks Project, *Divisors*, Section *Weil divisors*, the same statement for locally
  Noetherian schemes.
* Q. Liu, *Algebraic Geometry and Arithmetic Curves*, Section 9.1: distinct prime divisors on a
  regular surface meet properly, and their intersection number is a length.
-/

public section

open CategoryTheory TopologicalSpace Order

universe u

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

variable {X : Scheme.{u}}

/-- Near a point `x` of the closure of a codimension-one point `η` of a locally Noetherian scheme
with factorial local rings, the ideal of the closure of `η` is generated by a nonzerodivisor. -/
private lemma exists_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_ideal_eq_span_singleton
    [IsLocallyNoetherian X] [∀ x : X, IsDomain (X.presheaf.stalk x)]
    [∀ x : X, UniqueFactorizationMonoid (X.presheaf.stalk x)] {η : X} (hη : coheight η = 1)
    {x : X} (hx : x ∈ closure {η}) :
    ∃ V : X.affineOpens, x ∈ V.1 ∧ ∃ a : Γ(X, V), IsSMulRegular Γ(X, V) a ∧
      (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {η}, isClosed_closure⟩).ideal V = Ideal.span {a} := by
  obtain ⟨_, ⟨U, hU, rfl⟩, hxU, -⟩ :=
    X.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open (Set.mem_univ x) isOpen_univ
  have hU' : IsAffineOpen U := hU
  have : IsNoetherianRing Γ(X, U) := IsLocallyNoetherian.component_noetherian ⟨U, hU⟩
  have hηx : η ⤳ x := specializes_iff_mem_closure.mpr hx
  have hηU : η ∈ U := hηx.mem_open U.isOpen hxU
  -- On `U`, the ideal is the prime `q` of `η`, contained in the prime `P` of `x`.
  set P := hU'.primeIdealOf ⟨x, hxU⟩
  set q := hU'.primeIdealOf ⟨η, hηU⟩
  have hI := vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_ideal η ⟨U, hU⟩ hηU
  have hqP : q.asIdeal ≤ P.asIdeal := by
    refine (PrimeSpectrum.le_iff_specializes q P).mpr
      (hU'.fromSpec.isOpenEmbedding.isInducing.specializes_iff.mp ?_)
    rwa [hU'.fromSpec_primeIdealOf, hU'.fromSpec_primeIdealOf]
  have hdisj : Disjoint (P.asIdeal.primeCompl : Set Γ(X, U)) q.asIdeal :=
    Set.disjoint_left.mpr fun a ha haq ↦ ha (hqP haq)
  -- The stalk `S` at `x` is the localization of `Γ(X, U)` at `P`. There `q` generates a prime of
  -- height one, principal as `S` is factorial, with a generator coming from some `a ∈ q`.
  have := hU'.isLocalization_stalk ⟨x, hxU⟩
  set S := X.presheaf.stalk x
  let := X.presheaf.algebra_section_stalk ⟨x, hxU⟩
  have hQ : (q.asIdeal.map (algebraMap Γ(X, U) S)).IsPrime :=
    IsLocalization.isPrime_of_isPrime_disjoint P.asIdeal.primeCompl S _ q.2 hdisj
  have hqh : q.asIdeal.height = coheight (hU'.fromSpec q) :=
    (idealHeight_eq_coheight Γ(X, U) q).trans (coheight_eq_of_isOpenImmersion hU'.fromSpec).symm
  have hQh : (q.asIdeal.map (algebraMap Γ(X, U) S)).height = 1 := by
    rw [IsLocalization.height_map_of_disjoint P.asIdeal.primeCompl _ hdisj, hqh,
      hU'.fromSpec_primeIdealOf, hη]
  obtain ⟨c, hc : _ = Ideal.span {c}⟩ := UniqueFactorizationMonoid.isPrincipal_of_height_eq_one hQh
  obtain ⟨⟨⟨a, ha⟩, s⟩, has⟩ := (IsLocalization.mem_map_algebraMap_iff P.asIdeal.primeCompl S).mp
    (hc ▸ Ideal.mem_span_singleton_self c)
  have hQa : q.asIdeal.map (algebraMap Γ(X, U) S) = Ideal.span {algebraMap Γ(X, U) S a} := by
    rw [← has, Ideal.span_singleton_mul_right_unit (IsLocalization.map_units S s), ← hc]
  have hreg : IsSMulRegular S (algebraMap Γ(X, U) S a) := by
    refine IsSMulRegular.of_ne_zero fun ha0 ↦ Ideal.ne_bot_of_height_eq_one hQh ?_
    rw [hQa, ha0, Ideal.span_singleton_eq_bot]
  -- The local equation `a` spreads out to a basic open neighbourhood `D(g)` of `x`.
  obtain ⟨g, hgP, hgq, hga⟩ :=
    Ideal.exists_notMem_smul_le_span_singleton_of_map_eq (P := P.asIdeal) S hQa hreg
  have := hU'.isLocalization_basicOpen g
  refine ⟨X.affineBasicOpen (U := ⟨U, hU⟩) g, ?_, algebraMap Γ(X, U) Γ(X, X.basicOpen g) a,
    (IsLocalization.Away.isSMulRegular_algebraMap hga : IsSMulRegular Γ(X, X.basicOpen g) _), ?_⟩
  · -- `x` lies in `D(g)` because `g ∉ P`.
    rw [X.affineBasicOpen_coe, X.mem_basicOpen _ _ hxU]
    exact (IsLocalization.AtPrime.isUnit_to_map_iff S P.asIdeal g).mpr hgP
  · rw [← (vanishingIdeal _).map_ideal_basicOpen, hI]
    exact Ideal.map_algebraMap_away_eq_span_singleton ha hgq

/-- **Prime divisors on a locally factorial scheme are effective Cartier.** Let `X` be a locally
Noetherian scheme whose local rings are unique factorization domains, and `η` a point of
codimension one. Then the closure of `η`, with its reduced induced structure, is an effective
Cartier divisor. -/
theorem isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton [IsLocallyNoetherian X]
    [∀ x : X, IsDomain (X.presheaf.stalk x)]
    [∀ x : X, UniqueFactorizationMonoid (X.presheaf.stalk x)] {η : X} (hη : coheight η = 1) :
    (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {η}, isClosed_closure⟩).IsEffectiveCartier := by
  rw [isEffectiveCartier_iff]
  intro x
  by_cases hx : x ∈ closure {η}
  · exact exists_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_ideal_eq_span_singleton hη hx
  -- Away from the closure of `η`, the ideal is the unit ideal.
  obtain ⟨_, ⟨V, hV, rfl⟩, hxV, hVZ⟩ := X.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open hx
    isClosed_closure.isOpen_compl
  refine ⟨⟨V, hV⟩, hxV, 1, IsSMulRegular.one _, ?_⟩
  rw [Ideal.span_singleton_one, vanishingIdeal_ideal, Closeds.coe_mk, eq_top_iff]
  generalize_proofs hV'
  exact fun a _ ↦ (PrimeSpectrum.mem_vanishingIdeal _ _).mpr fun p hp ↦
    (hVZ (hV'.range_fromSpec ▸ Set.mem_range_self p) hp).elim

/-- **Prime divisors on a regular surface are effective Cartier.** On a regular scheme all of whose
points have codimension at most two, such as a regular model of a curve over a discrete valuation
ring, the closure of a codimension-one point, with its reduced induced structure, is an effective
Cartier divisor. -/
theorem isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_isRegular
    [TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.IsRegular X]
    (hX : ∀ x : X, coheight x ≤ 2) {η : X} (hη : coheight η = 1) :
    (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {η}, isClosed_closure⟩).IsEffectiveCartier :=
  have := fun x ↦ TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.IsRegular.uniqueFactorizationMonoid_stalk x (hX x)
  isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton hη

/-- **Distinct prime divisors meet properly.** Let `η ≠ η'` be codimension-one points of a scheme
`X` such that the closure of `η'`, with its reduced induced structure, is an effective Cartier
divisor. Then its inverse image on the closure of `η` is an effective Cartier divisor. -/
theorem IsEffectiveCartier.comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_ne
    {η η' : TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X}
    (hη' : (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η' : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩).IsEffectiveCartier)
    (hne : η ≠ η') :
    ((vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η' : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩).comap
      (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩).subschemeι).IsEffectiveCartier := by
  refine hη'.comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton fun h ↦ hne ?_
  rw [← SetLike.mem_coe, coe_support_vanishingIdeal, Closeds.coe_mk,
    ← specializes_iff_mem_closure] at h
  exact (TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint.eq_of_specializes h).symm

/-- **Distinct prime divisors on a regular surface meet properly.** On a regular scheme all of whose
points have codimension at most two, such as a regular model of a curve over a discrete valuation
ring, let `η ≠ η'` be codimension-one points. Then the inverse image of the closure of `η'` on the
closure of `η`, both with their reduced induced structures, is an effective Cartier divisor. -/
theorem isEffectiveCartier_comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_isRegular
    [TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.IsRegular X] (hX : ∀ x : X, coheight x ≤ 2)
    {η η' : TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X} (hne : η ≠ η') :
    ((vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η' : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩).comap
      (vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩).subschemeι).IsEffectiveCartier :=
  (isEffectiveCartier_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_isRegular hX η'.2)
    |>.comap_subschemeι_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_ne hne

section Principal

open IsLocalRing
open TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme (ord_algebraMap_stalk_nonneg
  ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_zero_iff ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_one
  exists_comap_stalkSpecializes_maximalIdeal_eq_span_singleton
  specializes_of_comap_stalkSpecializes_maximalIdeal_le)

open scoped Classical in
/-- The prime of `𝒪_{X,x}` lying under the maximal ideal of `𝒪_{X,η}` when `η` is a generization
of `x`, so that `𝒪_{X,η}` is the localization of `𝒪_{X,x}` at it, and the unit ideal otherwise.
For a codimension-one point `η` this is the local ideal at `x` of the prime divisor `closure {η}`
(`map_ideal_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton`). -/
private noncomputable def stalkIdeal (x η : X) : Ideal (X.presheaf.stalk x) :=
  if h : η ⤳ x then
    (maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk η)).comap (X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes h).hom
  else ⊤

private lemma stalkIdeal_of_specializes {x η : X} (h : η ⤳ x) :
    stalkIdeal x η =
      (maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk η)).comap (X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes h).hom :=
  dite_eq_left h

private lemma stalkIdeal_of_not_specializes {x η : X} (h : ¬ η ⤳ x) : stalkIdeal x η = ⊤ :=
  dite_eq_right h

private lemma stalkIdeal_ne_top {x η : X} (h : η ⤳ x) : stalkIdeal x η ≠ ⊤ := by
  rw [stalkIdeal_of_specializes h]
  exact (Ideal.comap_isPrime _ _).ne_top

variable {x : X} {U : X.affineOpens}

/-- On an affine open containing `x`, the ideal of the closure of a point `η` generates the prime
of `𝒪_{X,x}` of `η` when `η` is a generization of `x`, and the unit ideal otherwise. -/
private lemma map_ideal_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton (hxU : x ∈ U.1) (η : X) :
    ((vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {η}, isClosed_closure⟩).ideal U).map
      (X.presheaf.germ U.1 x hxU).hom = stalkIdeal x η := by
  by_cases h : η ⤳ x
  · rw [stalkIdeal_of_specializes h, map_ideal_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_specializes]
  · rw [stalkIdeal_of_not_specializes h,
      map_ideal_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton_of_not_specializes hxU h]

variable [IsIntegral X] [IsLocallyNoetherian X]

/-- **The divisor of a germ in a factorial local ring.** In the factorial local ring `𝒪_{X,x}`,
the principal ideal of a nonzero germ `b` is the product of the primes of the codimension-one
generizations `η` of `x`, each raised to the order of `b` at `η`. -/
-- By induction on the prime factorization of `b`: every prime factor `p` generates the prime of
-- exactly one codimension-one generization `η` of `x` (Krull's principal ideal theorem), and `p`
-- has order one at `η` and order zero at every other such point. The global decomposition
-- `ofIdealTop_span_singleton_eq_prod_vanishingIdeal` is then checked in each local ring `𝒪_{X,x}`.
private lemma span_singleton_eq_prod_stalkIdeal [UniqueFactorizationMonoid (X.presheaf.stalk x)]
    {b : X.presheaf.stalk x} (hb : b ≠ 0)
    (s : Finset (TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X))
    (hs : ∀ η : TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X,
      (η : X) ⤳ x → b ∈ stalkIdeal x η → η ∈ s) :
    Ideal.span {b} = ∏ η ∈ s,
      stalkIdeal x η ^ (X.ord (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField b) η).toNat := by
  classical
  induction b using UniqueFactorizationMonoid.induction_on_prime generalizing s with
  | h₁ => exact absurd rfl hb
  | h₂ u hu =>
    -- A unit has order zero at every generization, and every other factor is the unit ideal.
    rw [Ideal.span_singleton_eq_top.mpr hu, ← Ideal.one_eq_top, eq_comm]
    refine Finset.prod_eq_one fun η _ ↦ ?_
    by_cases h : (η : X) ⤳ x
    · rw [(ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_zero_iff η.2 h hu.ne_zero).mpr fun hmem ↦
        stalkIdeal_ne_top h (Ideal.eq_top_of_isUnit_mem _ (stalkIdeal_of_specializes h ▸ hmem) hu),
        Int.toNat_zero, pow_zero]
    · rw [stalkIdeal_of_not_specializes h, Ideal.top_pow, Ideal.one_eq_top]
  | h₃ a p ha hp ih =>
    -- The prime `p` contributes exactly one factor, the prime of its codimension-one point.
    obtain ⟨η₀, hη₀1, hη₀x, hη₀⟩ := exists_comap_stalkSpecializes_maximalIdeal_eq_span_singleton hp
    rw [← stalkIdeal_of_specializes hη₀x] at hη₀
    lift η₀ to TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X using hη₀1
    have hη₀s : η₀ ∈ s :=
      hs η₀ hη₀x (hη₀ ▸ Ideal.mem_span_singleton.mpr (dvd_mul_right p a))
    have hp0 : algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField p ≠ 0 :=
      (map_ne_zero_iff _ (IsFractionRing.injective _ _)).mpr hp.ne_zero
    have ha0 : algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField a ≠ 0 :=
      (map_ne_zero_iff _ (IsFractionRing.injective _ _)).mpr ha
    have key : ∀ η ∈ s, stalkIdeal x η ^
        (X.ord (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField (p * a)) η).toNat =
        (if η₀ = η then Ideal.span {p} else 1) * stalkIdeal x η ^
          (X.ord (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField a) η).toNat := by
      intro η _
      by_cases h : (η : X) ⤳ x
      · rw [map_mul, X.ord_mul hp0 ha0,
          Int.toNat_add (ord_algebraMap_stalk_nonneg η.2 h p)
            (ord_algebraMap_stalk_nonneg η.2 h a), pow_add]
        by_cases he : η₀ = η
        · subst he
          rw [ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_one η₀.2 h (stalkIdeal_of_specializes h ▸ hη₀),
            ite_eq_left rfl, hη₀,
            Int.toNat_one, pow_one]
        · rw [(ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_zero_iff η.2 h hp.ne_zero).mpr fun hmem ↦ he
            (TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint.eq_of_specializes
              (specializes_of_comap_stalkSpecializes_maximalIdeal_le hη₀x h
                (by rwa [← stalkIdeal_of_specializes hη₀x, hη₀, Ideal.span_singleton_le_iff_mem]))),
            ite_eq_right he, Int.toNat_zero, pow_zero, one_mul]
      · have he : η₀ ≠ η := fun he ↦ h (he ▸ hη₀x)
        rw [stalkIdeal_of_not_specializes h, Ideal.top_pow, Ideal.top_pow, ite_eq_right he,
          one_mul]
    rw [Finset.prod_congr rfl key, Finset.prod_mul_distrib, Finset.prod_ite_eq, ite_eq_left hη₀s,
      ← ih ha s fun η hηx hmem ↦ hs η hηx (Ideal.mul_mem_left _ p hmem),
      Ideal.span_singleton_mul_span_singleton]

/-- An integral scheme is nonempty, so its global functions have germs in the function field. -/
local instance : Nonempty (⊤ : X.Opens) := ⟨⟨genericPoint X, trivial⟩⟩

/-- **Principal divisors on a locally factorial scheme.** Let `X` be a locally Noetherian integral
scheme whose local rings are unique factorization domains, and let `f` be a nonzero global
function. Then the ideal sheaf generated by `f` is the product of the ideal sheaves of the prime
divisors `closure {η}`, each raised to the order of vanishing of `f` at `η`, over any finite set
of codimension-one points containing those at which `f` vanishes. In other words, the effective
Cartier divisor `div f` is the sum `Σ ord_η(f) · closure {η}` of prime divisors, as subschemes and
not only as Weil divisors. -/
theorem ofIdealTop_span_singleton_eq_prod_vanishingIdeal
    [∀ x : X, UniqueFactorizationMonoid (X.presheaf.stalk x)] {f : Γ(X, ⊤)} (hf : f ≠ 0)
    (s : Finset (TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X))
    (hs : ∀ η : TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X,
      0 < X.ord (X.germToFunctionField ⊤ f) η → η ∈ s) :
    ofIdealTop (Ideal.span {f}) = ∏ η ∈ s,
      vanishingIdeal ⟨closure {(η : X)}, isClosed_closure⟩ ^
        (X.ord (X.germToFunctionField ⊤ f) η).toNat := by
  refine IdealSheafData.ext (funext fun U ↦ ?_)
  let φ : X.IdealSheafData →* Ideal Γ(X, U) :=
    { toFun := fun J ↦ J.ideal U, map_one' := by simp, map_mul' := fun _ _ ↦ rfl }
  have hprod : ∀ (I : TauCeti.AlgebraicGeometry.CodimensionOnePoint X → X.IdealSheafData),
      (∏ η ∈ s, I η).ideal U = ∏ η ∈ s, (I η).ideal U := fun I ↦ map_prod φ I s
  rw [ofIdealTop_ideal, hprod]
  refine U.2.ideal_ext_iff.mpr fun x hxU ↦ ?_
  rw [Ideal.map_map, Ideal.map_span, Set.image_singleton, ← CommRingCat.hom_comp,
    TopCat.Presheaf.germ_res, ← Ideal.mapHom_apply, map_prod]
  simp only [Ideal.mapHom_apply, ideal_pow, Pi.pow_apply, Ideal.map_pow,
    map_ideal_vanishingIdeal_closure_singleton hxU]
  have hx : x ∈ (⊤ : X.Opens) := trivial
  have hgerm : X.presheaf.germ ⊤ x hx f ≠ 0 := fun h0 ↦
    hf (germ_injective_of_isIntegral X x hx (h0.trans (map_zero _).symm))
  have hord : X.germToFunctionField ⊤ f =
      algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk x) X.functionField (X.presheaf.germ ⊤ x hx f) :=
    (X.algebraMap_germ_eq_germToFunctionField hx f).symm
  rw [hord]
  refine span_singleton_eq_prod_stalkIdeal hgerm s fun η hηx hmem ↦ hs η ?_
  have h0 := ord_algebraMap_stalk_nonneg η.2 hηx (X.presheaf.germ ⊤ x hx f)
  have hne := (ord_algebraMap_stalk_eq_zero_iff η.2 hηx hgerm).not_left.mpr
    (stalkIdeal_of_specializes hηx ▸ hmem)
  rw [hord]
  omega

